    <article class="prose">
      <p class="lead">
        Virginia workers’ compensation pays your medical treatment and part of your lost wages when
        you are hurt on the job. You do not have to prove your employer did anything wrong. Wage
        checks are generally two-thirds of your average weekly wage, up to a yearly maximum. Tell your
        employer in writing within 30 days, and file a claim with the Virginia Workers’ Compensation
        Commission within two years of the accident, although exceptions apply.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pathfinder Injury Law handles only Virginia
        <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/virginia-workers-compensation/">workers’ compensation</a>
        claims. This page explains who qualifies, what benefits you can get, how a claim works, and
        what to do if the insurance company says no.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cases">Cases We Handle</h2>
      <ul class="topic-grid">
        {cases.map((c) => (
          <li>
            <a href={c.href}>
              <span class="topic-circle"><TopicIcon name={c.icon} /></span>
              <span class="topic-label">{c.label}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        We can help at any stage, from reporting the injury to a hearing before the Virginia Workers’
        Compensation Commission (VWC).
      </p>

      <h3>Workers’ Comp Denials</h3>
      <p>
        A denial is not the end of your claim. You have the right to ask the Commission for a hearing.
        Common reasons insurance companies deny claims:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Not enough evidence of the injury or how it happened</li>
        <li>No medical treatment after the injury</li>
        <li>Late notice to the employer</li>
        <li>A claim filed after the two-year deadline</li>
        <li>An adjuster who simply did not pay attention to the claim</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Depending on the reason, you may still be able to get benefits. Here’s
        <a href="/how-to-appeal-a-workers-comp-denial-in-virginia/">how to appeal a workers’ comp denial in Virginia</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Construction Accidents</h3>
      <p>
        Falls, falling objects, and equipment accidents on construction sites cause brain injuries,
        neck and back injuries, broken bones, and joint injuries. To be covered, the accident must
        arise out of and in the course of your job and cause a sudden, identifiable, structural or
        mechanical change to your body. See our
        <a href="/virginia-construction-accident-lawyer/">construction accident page</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Factory Accidents</h3>
      <p>
        Machinery and plant accidents can cause amputations, crush injuries, spinal injuries, and
        joint injuries that leave a worker unable to do the job. See our
        <a href="/virginia-factory-accident-lawyer/">factory accident page</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Work Vehicle Accidents</h3>
      <p>
        Crashes in delivery vans, company cars, forklifts, and heavy equipment are common. You do not
        have to prove who caused the crash. You do have to show the accident arose out of and in the
        course of your job and caused a sudden, identifiable, structural or mechanical change to your
        body. If another driver caused it, you may also have a separate claim against that driver.
        See our <a href="/car-accident-at-work-in-virginia/">work vehicle accident page</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Back Injuries</h3>
      <p>
        Back injuries from a fall, falling equipment, or a lifting accident can keep you from almost
        any work. If the accident qualifies, workers’ comp pays for treatment and for temporary or
        permanent disability. See our
        <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/virginia-back-injury-lawyer/">back injury page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="eligible">Who Qualifies for Workers’ Comp in Virginia?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>You generally qualify if you are an employee, your employer is covered, and you were
        hurt in an accident that arose out of and in the course of your job.</strong>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your employer is covered.</strong> Virtually all employers with three or more
          employees regularly in service in Virginia are required to carry workers’ compensation
          insurance (<a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter1/section65.2-101/" rel="noopener">Va. Code § 65.2-101</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You are an employee.</strong> Employers sometimes label workers as independent
          contractors (1099) when they are really employees. The Commission may hold a hearing to
          decide whether you have the functions or attributes of an employee, regardless of how your
          employer described you. Here’s
          <a href="/how-to-qualify-for-workers-comp-in-virginia/">how to qualify for workers’ comp in Virginia</a>
          and <a href="/what-to-do-when-your-employer-lists-you-as-an-independent-contractor/">what to do if you’re listed as an independent contractor</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The injury came from an accident at work.</strong> It must arise out of and in the
          course of your employment and cause a sudden, identifiable, structural or mechanical change
          to your body.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="pi-vs-wc">Workers’ Comp vs. a Personal Injury Claim</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Workers’ comp is no-fault: you do not have to prove your employer or anyone else was to
        blame.</strong> The trade-off is that it pays only certain things: medical treatment, part of
        your lost wages, and benefits for permanent damage to certain body parts. It does not pay for
        pain and suffering or mental anguish, which a personal injury case can.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>You cannot sue your employer or your coworkers</strong> for an accident covered by
          workers’ comp
          (<a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter3/section65.2-307/" rel="noopener">Va. Code § 65.2-307</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You may be able to sue an outside party</strong> who caused the injury, such as a
          negligent driver, as long as they are not a coworker or in the same trade, business, or
          occupation as your employer
          (<a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter3/section65.2-309/" rel="noopener">Va. Code § 65.2-309</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rarely,</strong> if the Commission holds a hearing and decides your accident did not
          arise out of and in the course of your employment, you could potentially sue your employer.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Pathfinder Injury Law handles only Virginia workers’ compensation claims. We do not handle
        personal injury lawsuits.
      </p>

      <h2 id="benefits">What Does Virginia Workers’ Comp Pay?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Workers’ comp pays medical treatment, part of your lost wages, benefits for permanent
        impairment, and, after a fatal accident, death benefits for the family.</strong> Employers pay
        for the system through insurance premiums and claims, and the Virginia Workers’ Compensation
        Commission handles claims and decides disputes. For a full rundown, see
        <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/what-does-workers-comp-cover-in-virginia/">what workers’ comp covers in Virginia</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Medical Treatment</h3>
      <p>
        The insurance company is required to pay for medical treatment that is reasonable, necessary,
        and causally related to your work accident
        (<a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter6/section65.2-603/" rel="noopener">Va. Code § 65.2-603</a>).
        That does not mean it always will. That can include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Hospital care and doctor visits, including specialists</li>
        <li>Surgery, physical therapy, and rehabilitation</li>
        <li>Prescription medications</li>
        <li>Orthopedic and prosthetic equipment</li>
        <li>In the right circumstances, changes to your home or vehicle</li>
        <li>Mileage reimbursement for travel to and from medical appointments</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Wage Loss and Disability Benefits</h3>
      <p>
        Wage checks start on the eighth day you are out of work, and the first seven days are paid
        back if you are out more than three weeks. Here’s
        <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/when-does-virginia-workers-comp-start-paying/">when Virginia workers’ comp starts paying</a>
        and <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/how-is-workers-comp-calculated-in-virginia/">how benefits are calculated</a>.
        There are four types of disability benefits:
      </p>
      <div class="type-grid">
        <div class="type-card">
          <h4><abbr title="Temporary Total Disability">TTD</abbr>Temporary Total Disability</h4>
          <p>
            If a doctor says you cannot return to work at all for a temporary period because of your
            injuries, you may receive two-thirds of the pre-injury average weekly wage you received
            during the year immediately prior to your accident, up to a maximum weekly compensation
            rate.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="type-card">
          <h4><abbr title="Temporary Partial Disability">TPD</abbr>Temporary Partial Disability</h4>
          <p>
            You may receive benefits to help offset loss of income when your doctor only allows you to
            return to work with restrictions (often called “light duty”) and you are earning less than
            you were before you got hurt. The amount paid in these situations is two-thirds of the
            difference between what you were earning before you got hurt, and what you are earning now
            that you are injured and working light duty.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="type-card">
          <h4><abbr title="Permanent Partial Disability">PPD</abbr>Permanent Partial Disability</h4>
          <p>
            Once your doctor says you have recovered as much as you are going to
            (<a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/what-is-maximum-medical-improvement-in-virginia-workers-comp-claims/">maximum medical improvement</a>),
            you may be rated for permanent impairment. If the rating is above zero for a body part the
            law covers, you may be paid for that permanent loss under a statutory formula.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="type-card">
          <h4><abbr title="Permanent Total Disability">PTD</abbr>Permanent Total Disability</h4>
          <p>
            When your injuries remain so severe that you are unable to use two or more injured body
            parts at all in any sort of gainful employment,
            <a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter5/section65.2-503/" rel="noopener">as defined by Virginia law</a>,
            you may be eligible for permanent total disability benefits. These benefits can also be
            available for severe permanent loss of sight or hearing in both eyes or ears, and for brain
            injuries that render you permanently unable to engage in gainful employment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Death Benefits</h3>
      <p>
        Workers’ comp can provide death benefits to the family of a worker who loses their life because
        of a workplace injury. These benefits can pay for a limited amount of funeral expenses, medical
        bills incurred before the death, and lost wages for certain periods of time for certain
        survivors or dependents who meet statutory criteria.
      </p>

      <h2 id="filing">How to File a Workers’ Comp Claim in Virginia</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Tell your employer in writing.</strong> The Virginia Workers’ Compensation Act
          requires notice to your employer within thirty days of the accident, although exceptions
          exist. Written notice is much easier to prove than verbal notice. Say that an accident
          happened, that you were hurt, and how it happened, and keep a copy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Get medical treatment</strong> and tell every doctor the injury happened at work.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>File a claim with the Commission.</strong> It must be on file with the Virginia
          Workers’ Compensation Commission within two years of your date of accident, although
          exceptions apply. File even if the insurance company accepts your injury and starts paying:
          a filed claim protects your two-year filing deadline (the statute of limitations) if
          anything goes wrong later. You can file online through the Commission’s WebFile system, in
          person at a Commission office, by mail, or by fax. Here’s
          <a href="/how-to-file-a-workers-comp-claim/">how to file a workers’ comp claim</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Watch for the insurance company’s answer.</strong> After you file, the Commission
          will order your employer’s insurance company to respond to your claim within 30 days. It may
          accept the claim or deny it.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="disputes">What If the Insurance Company Denies or Disputes Your Claim?</h2>
      <p>
        If you have received a denial of your workers’ comp claim, you have the right to dispute that
        decision and take your claim to a hearing before the Virginia Workers’ Compensation
        Commission. You can ask for a hearing no matter what the insurance company decides, including
        when it accepts the claim but refuses to pay for certain treatment or benefits.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the hearing, a deputy commissioner hears the evidence and decides whether benefits are
        due. Your lawyer gathers the medical records and other evidence beforehand, presents your case,
        and questions the witnesses. Here’s
        <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/how-to-prepare-for-a-virginia-workers-comp-hearing/">how to prepare for a workers’ comp hearing</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="settlement">Settling a Virginia Workers’ Comp Claim</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>A settlement is optional. Nobody can force you to settle, and nobody can force the
        insurance company to offer one.</strong> The Commission must approve every settlement
        (<a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter7/section65.2-701/" rel="noopener">Va. Code § 65.2-701</a>).
        The amount has to account for future medical care and lost earning power, because after a
        full and final settlement you cannot come back for more.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most cases settle with a <strong>full and final settlement</strong>: a lump sum that closes your
        entire case, top to bottom, with no future benefit entitlement of any kind, whether for wage
        loss, medical bills, vocational rehab, permanent partial disability benefits, or any other
        benefits. Settling your case closes everything and ends benefits. Settlements that leave
        medical benefits open are exceedingly rare.
      </p>
      <p>
        More on <a href="/workers-comp-settlement-in-virginia/">workers’ comp settlements in Virginia</a>
        and <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/how-to-calculate-a-virginia-workers-comp-settlement/">how a settlement is calculated</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="prepare">What to Bring to a Consultation</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Medical records about your injuries</li>
        <li>Messages with your employer, including the injury report if you made one</li>
        <li>Messages with the insurance company</li>
        <li>Anything you received from or sent to the Commission</li>
        <li>Pay stubs or other proof of your usual income</li>
        <li>Photos, notes, and anything else about the accident and your injuries</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="why-hire">Why Hire a Virginia Workers’ Comp Lawyer?</h2>
      <p>
        Your employer’s insurance company has handled far more workers’ comp claims than you have. A
        lawyer evens that out: we deal with the adjuster, gather the medical evidence, push for the
        treatment your doctor recommends, and take the claim to a hearing if needed. There is no fee up
        front.
      </p>

      <div class="pull">
        <h2>Injured in Virginia? Find Out How We Can Help.</h2>
        <p>
          Pathfinder Injury Law handles only Virginia workers’ compensation, with offices in the
          Richmond area and Chincoteague, and clients across the state, including
          <a href="/richmond-workers-comp-lawyer/">Richmond</a>,
          <a href="/hanover-county-workers-comp-lawyer/">Hanover</a>,
          <a href="/chesterfield-county-workers-comp-lawyer/">Chesterfield</a>,
          <a href="/norfolk-workers-comp-lawyer/">Norfolk</a>,
          <a href="/virginia-beach-workers-comp-lawyer/">Virginia Beach</a>,
          <a href="/newport-news-workers-comp-lawyer/">Newport News</a>,
          <a href="/fairfax-county-workers-comp-lawyer/">Fairfax</a>, and
