          free. Call <a href={site.phoneHref}>804-505-0633</a> or use our
          <a href="#contact">online contact form</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How long does workers’ comp last in Virginia?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              Wage benefits last as long as you remain disabled and the claim stays open, but total
              compensation is generally capped at 500 weeks, with exceptions such as permanent total
              incapacity
              (<a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter5/section65.2-518/" rel="noopener">Va. Code § 65.2-518</a>).
              Here’s <a href="/how-long-can-a-workers-comp-claim-stay-open/">how long a workers’ comp claim can stay open</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is workers’ comp required in Virginia?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              Yes, for virtually all employers with three or more employees regularly in service in
              Virginia. If your employer should have had coverage and didn’t, you may still have options,
              so talk with a lawyer.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I get workers’ comp if the accident was my fault?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              Usually, yes. Fault generally doesn’t matter in Virginia Workers’ Compensation claims.
              Even if your own mistake or negligence caused your work injury, you can usually still
              recover benefits. However, a willful failure to use a safety appliance or perform a
              statutory duty, or a willful breach of a known, reasonable rule of the employer, can bar
              recovery. The employer has the burden of proof.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long do I have to file a workers’ comp claim in Virginia?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              Generally two years from the date of the accident, although exceptions apply. Miss it and
              you can lose your right to benefits. Separately, you must notify your employer within 30
              days. File the claim even if the insurance company is already paying.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can my employer fire me for filing a workers’ comp claim?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              Virginia law prohibits an employer from discharging an employee solely because the
              employee has filed or intends to file a Workers’ Comp claim, or has testified or is about
              to testify in a proceeding under the Act
              (<a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter3/section65.2-308/" rel="noopener">Va. Code § 65.2-308</a>).
              Other actions, such as a demotion or discipline, may or may not be covered. More on
              <a href="/employer-retaliation-and-your-workers-comp-rights/">employer retaliation and your workers’ comp rights</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much does a workers’ comp lawyer cost in Virginia?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              The Virginia Workers’ Compensation Commission must approve all attorney fees in Workers’
              Comp cases. We are generally paid on contingency, meaning we collect attorney fees from the
              benefits we obtain for our clients. Costs we advance are subject to reimbursement from the
              client.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long do Virginia workers’ comp settlements take?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              There is no set timeline. It depends on your medical recovery and on whether the insurance
              company disputes the claim. The Commission must approve any settlement before it is final.
              Here’s more on
              <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/how-long-do-virginia-workers-comp-settlements-take/">how long Virginia workers’ comp settlements take</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I get workers’ comp and Social Security Disability at the same time?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              Some people receive both, but one can reduce the other, and the way a workers’ comp
              settlement is written can change how much you keep. More on
              <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/can-i-receive-workers-comp-and-social-security-disability-at-the-same-time/">workers’ comp and Social Security Disability</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need a lawyer for a Virginia workers’ comp claim?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              You aren’t required to have one. But the insurance company handles claims like yours every
              day, and the claim runs on its own rules: a 30-day notice deadline, a two-year filing
              deadline, medical proof that the accident caused the injury, recorded statements, light-duty
              offers, and settlement terms that cannot be undone. Call Pathfinder Injury Law at
              <a href={site.phoneHref}>804-505-0633</a> or <a href="#contact">contact us</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What parts of Virginia do you serve?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              We take clients from all over Virginia, with offices in the Richmond area and
              Chincoteague. See our <a href="/about-us/#areas-served">areas served</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </article>

